  "heat": 2,
  "text": "Paul told a whole church to give Phoebe whatever she needed. Romans sixteen. Paul opens the final chapter of the most theologically dense letter in the New Testament with a personal commendation. I commend unto you Phebe our sister, which is a servant of the church which is at Cenchrea. The word behind servant there is diakonos, which elsewhere gets translated deacon or minister, and translators have gone back and forth on it for centuries. Then he asks the Roman church to receive her in the Lord, as becometh saints, and to assist her in whatsoever business she hath need of you. Give her whatever she needs. And then his reason. For she hath been a succourer of many, and of myself also. A patron. A protector. She had backed Paul personally. Now, you will often hear that Phoebe carried the letter to Rome. That is not stated in the text. It is a very old and very reasonable inference from the fact that she is commended first, at the front of the greetings, in the way a letter carrier would be. Reasonable inference, not chapter and verse.",
